Software Requirement Specification

**TradeSage AI**

*CE363: Project-III | CHARUSAT*

# 1. Introduction

## 1.1 Purpose

This document specifies the functional and non-functional requirements for TradeSage AI, a personal trading intelligence platform combining portfolio tracking with an ML-driven signal engine for stocks and cryptocurrency.

## 1.2 Scope

TradeSage AI allows a user to track holdings, receive explainable direction-based trading signals (up/down/neutral), backtest those signals honestly, and monitor performance via a dashboard. It is explicitly a decision-support tool, not a financial advisory or trade-execution system.

## 1.3 Intended Audience

Internal project guide (CE363 evaluation), and the developer (for implementation reference).

## 1.4 Definitions

- **Signal: **A directional prediction (up/down/neutral) for an asset over a defined horizon (default: 3 days)

- **Backtest: **Simulated historical evaluation of signal performance

- **Sharpe ratio / drawdown / win-rate: **Risk-adjusted performance metrics, defined in Section 4 (Backtesting)

# 2. Overall Description

## 2.1 Actors

- **Trader/User: **the primary actor; tracks portfolio, views signals, configures watchlist, reviews backtests

- **Admin: **same physical person as Trader/User (solo project), but a distinct role accessed via a config panel: manages tracked assets, retrain schedule, data source health

## 2.2 Assumptions & Constraints

- No real-money execution; all trades are manually logged or CSV-imported

- Free-tier data sources only (yfinance, Binance public API, NewsAPI, Reddit/PRAW) — subject to rate limits, which constrains refresh frequency

- Single-user deployment is sufficient (multi-user auth is a nice-to-have, not required)

## 2.3 Use Case Summary

| **Use Case** | **Actor** | **Related FRs** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Register / Log in | Trader/User | FR-1.1–1.3 |
| Add/import holdings | Trader/User | FR-2.1, 2.2 |
| View portfolio P&L | Trader/User | FR-2.3, 2.4 |
| View trading signal | Trader/User | FR-3.1–3.5 |
| Run/view backtest | Trader/User | FR-4.1–4.3 |
| Manage watchlist | Trader/User | FR-5.1, 5.2 |
| View dashboard | Trader/User | FR-6.1–6.3 |
| Manage tracked assets | Admin | FR-7.1 |
| Configure retrain schedule | Admin | FR-7.2 |
| Monitor data source health | Admin | FR-7.3 |

# 3. Functional Requirements

## FR-1: Authentication

**FR-1.1: **User can register with email/password

**FR-1.2: **User can log in/out

**FR-1.3: **Passwords stored hashed (never plaintext)

## FR-2: Portfolio Tracker

**FR-2.1: **User can manually add a holding (asset, quantity, buy price, date)

**FR-2.2: **User can import holdings via CSV

**FR-2.3: **System fetches live price for each held asset (stocks via yfinance, crypto via Binance public API)

**FR-2.4: **System calculates and displays unrealized P&L per holding and portfolio-wide

## FR-3: Signal Engine

**FR-3.1: **System computes technical indicators (RSI, MACD, moving averages, volume) per tracked asset

**FR-3.2: **System computes a sentiment score from news headlines (stocks) or Reddit posts (crypto)

**FR-3.3: **System generates a direction prediction (up/down/neutral) over a 3-day horizon using two models: a baseline (Logistic Regression or Random Forest) and an LSTM/GRU, shown side by side

**FR-3.4: **System displays a confidence score and top contributing features (via SHAP or feature importance) per signal

**FR-3.5: **System never predicts an exact future price — direction only

## FR-4: Backtesting Module

**FR-4.1: **System simulates signal performance over historical data

**FR-4.2: **System reports Sharpe ratio, win-rate, and maximum drawdown — never leads with raw accuracy

**FR-4.3: **Backtest results are viewable per-asset and in aggregate

## FR-5: Watchlist & Alerts (stretch goal)

**FR-5.1: **User can add assets to a watchlist without holding them

**FR-5.2: **System notifies user when a watchlisted asset's signal changes

## FR-6: Dashboard

**FR-6.1: **Displays portfolio overview (holdings, P&L, allocation)

**FR-6.2: **Displays signal history vs. actual outcome per asset (feedback loop)

**FR-6.3: **Displays stock vs. crypto comparison view

## FR-7: Admin/Config Panel (stretch goal)

**FR-7.1: **Manage list of tracked assets

**FR-7.2: **Configure model retrain schedule

**FR-7.3: **Monitor data source health (API failures, stale data)

# 4. External Interface Requirements

- **User Interface: **Web-based, browser-accessed (HTML/CSS/JS), responsive enough for laptop use (mobile not a target for this scope)

- **Software Interfaces: **yfinance (stock price data), Binance public API (crypto price data), NewsAPI (stock sentiment source), Reddit API via PRAW (crypto sentiment source)

- **Communication Interfaces: **HTTPS for all client-server communication; scheduled backend jobs (cron/Celery) poll external APIs — no real-time push/websocket requirement for this scope

- **Hardware Interfaces: **None (no embedded/IoT dependency)

# 5. Data Requirements (Key Entities)

- **User: **credentials, profile

- **Holding: **asset symbol, quantity, buy price, buy date, asset type (stock/crypto)

- **Signal: **asset, timestamp, predicted direction, confidence score, contributing features, model used (baseline/LSTM)

- **BacktestResult: **asset, date range, Sharpe ratio, win-rate, max drawdown

- **WatchlistItem: **asset symbol, date added (stretch goal)

# 6. Non-Functional Requirements

- **NFR-1 (Performance): **Dashboard should load portfolio + latest signals within 3 seconds under normal conditions

- **NFR-2 (Reliability): **System should gracefully handle data source API failures (show stale-data warning, not crash)

- **NFR-3 (Usability): **Signals must display confidence + contributing factors — no unexplained black-box output

- **NFR-4 (Security): **Passwords hashed; no plaintext credentials stored

- **NFR-5 (Maintainability): **Code follows CHARUSAT Coding Standard v1.0 (naming conventions, module headers) throughout

- **NFR-6 (Honesty/Ethical): **UI must display a persistent disclaimer that signals are decision-support, not financial advice; backtested metrics must never be presented as guarantees of future performance

# 7. Limitations

Market noise, non-stationarity of financial time series, survivorship bias in backtesting, and no guarantee of future performance are acknowledged constraints — not treated as solvable, but as honestly reported boundaries of the system.